 Fixed/improved with the very nice help of GPT-5.5 and ChatGPT-5.5 thanks.
+
+## 160 - (Fixed) Likely inherited AI upgrade-budget issue: normal upgrades could overshoot the remaining budget and leave the AI almost broke
+
+Screenshots/files for this issue: [google drive folder link](https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1p033n4oF_v-btMYwng7iJzowNaXtu2sC?usp=sharing).
+
+This looks likely inherited from base/K-Mod/AdvCiv-style upgrade flow because the old logic in `CvPlayerAI::AI_doTurnUnitsPost` already used a 4-pass upgrade loop and checked only whether spending so far was below the upgrade budget before attempting the next upgrade.
+
+The initial aggregate BBAI log showed suspicious upgrade spending:
+
+- 305 upgrade-spend events.
+- 23 events overspent the listed upgrade budget.
+- 49 events left the AI with <= 10 gold.
+- 110 events left the AI with <= 25 gold.
+- 183 events left the AI with <= 50 gold.
+- 17 events both overspent the budget and left <= 50 gold.
+- No-active-war upgrades were especially suspicious: 151 events, 20 budget overspends, 62 ending with <= 25 gold, and 29 ending with <= 10 gold.
+
+The old aggregate line did not show which pass or unit caused the spending, so diagnostics were added:
+
+- `UPGRADE_BUDGET_DETAIL`: starting gold, gold targets, upgrade budget, total upgrade need, focus-war, war-plan, financial-trouble, city/unit count, and era.
+- `UPGRADE_SPEND_DETAIL`: one line per paid upgrade, including pass name, unit id/type/AI/domain, plot/city, best-defender and danger flags, cost, gold before/after, budget, and context.
+- `UPGRADE_PASS_SUMMARY`: per-pass eligible units, paid upgrades, rough budget skips, and pass spending.
+- `UPGRADE_BUDGET_RESULT`: final spending result paired with the old aggregate line.
+
+The diagnostic run showed the main problem was not emergency defense upgrades:
+
+- Pass 0 `impassable`: 18 upgrades, 2777 gold, 1 overspent-after upgrade.
+- Pass 1 `city_defender_or_danger`: 168 upgrades, 16107 gold, 2 overspent-after upgrades.
+- Pass 2 `transport_or_escort_sea`: 1 upgrade, 197 gold, 0 overspent-after upgrades.
+- Pass 3 `normal`: 787 upgrades, 82937 gold, 37 overspent-after upgrades.
+
+The specific bug-like pattern was that pass 3 only checked `spent so far < budget`. If spending was still below budget before an upgrade, the AI could start another expensive normal upgrade even when that upgrade could not fit inside the remaining budget. Example from the reviewed log: England on T342 had 499 gold and a 279 budget, upgraded twice for 237 each, spent 474 total, and ended at 25 gold.
+
+Financial-trouble cases confirmed the same pattern. Example: England on T346 had `financialTrouble=1`, spent 237 with budget 59, and went from 246 gold to 9 gold on a pass 3 normal upgrade.
+
+Fix:
+
+- Keep pass 0/1 emergency-ish upgrades uncapped.
+- For pass 2/3, pass a maximum allowed upgrade price into `CvUnitAI::AI_upgrade`, equal to the remaining upgrade budget.
+- `CvUnitAI::AI_upgrade` now ignores upgrade candidates whose `upgradePrice` exceeds that max price, while otherwise preserving the old candidate iteration and value logic for affordable upgrades.
+- Skip pass 3 normal upgrades entirely if the AI started in financial trouble and is not focus-war.
+
+Follow-up BBAI comparison showed the intended result:
+
+- Upgrade-spend turns: 362 -> 355.
+- Individual paid upgrades: 974 -> 915.
+- Total upgrade gold spent: 102018 -> 84095.
+- Overspent budget turns: 40 -> 1.
+- Pass 3 overspent upgrades: 37 -> 0.
+- Financial-trouble spends: 33 -> 4.
+- Financial-trouble overspend: 7 -> 0.
+
+The single remaining overspend was pass 0 impassable: Mali on T176 spent 408 against a 365 budget and still kept 165 gold. Since pass 0 is intentionally uncapped and this did not match the old pass 3 low-gold pattern, it was accepted.
+
+Low final gold can still happen in focus-war / any-war-plan contexts. That is a separate possible follow-up about preserving a cash reserve during war preparation, not the same bug as pass 3 normal upgrades overshooting their remaining budget.
+
+Fixed with the very nice help of GPT-5.5 and ChatGPT-5.5 thanks.

diff --git a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
index e7298cb27..091bae49a 100644
--- a/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
+++ b/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Main_Changes_Guide.md
@@ -320,6 +320,7 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5943+) **Unit upgrades - prefer the important domain for the map:** AI unit-upgrade value now optionally uses the existing **SAS map-script heaviness** logic to **relatively value** unit upgrades (not a hard ban unless relative value is set high): the real gold price is unchanged; this makes the AI think actual upgrade cost is higher for these unit upgrades. As a result, on **land-heavy maps, naval upgrades are treated as lower priority**, so the AI is more likely to **spend gold upgrading land units first** instead of modernizing old ships that have little strategic value on that map. On **naval-heavy maps, the same idea can apply in reverse for land upgrades**.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5947+) **Remote captured-city attack stacks:** large AI attack **stacks in faraway captured cities no longer let upgrade waiting override a ready offensive target**. If the stack is large enough, has a valid target, and the target is not too strong, it keeps using the existing attack movement logic **instead of parking most of the empire's army far from its core** because several units could upgrade, which was very problematic. In the BBAI-log sample, the Celtic Boston trapped-stack pattern was visible from T156 to T256; the stricter remote-trap diagnostic confirmed it from T172 to T214, with up to 41 units / 62% of Celtic military parked in a faraway captured city while still having ready targets. See [KI#155](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#155---fixed-base-advciv-issue-remote-captured-city-attack-stacks-could-park-for-many-turns-because-upgrade-waiting-overrode-a-ready-offensive-target).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5948+) **Ready attack stacks stop parking for unavailable upgrades:** if a city-attack stack is ready, the target is not too strong, and **0 units can upgrade now**, the AI now keeps moving toward the target instead of waiting for possible future upgrades. This fixes real offensive freezes: in one BBAI-log sample, USA Washington waited from **T147-T181** with up to **59 units / 69% of total military** while targets were only **3-5 turns** away; Mali Gao waited from **T233-T255** with up to **42 units / 41% of total military**. The AI still waits for real immediate upgrades: a USA Seattle stack waited while **6 units could upgrade now**, then attacked/moved once that number became **0**. This is thought to be much stronger, because it uses the current military advantage before enemies catch up; old units can be spent in war instead of sitting around draining upkeep, needing future upgrade gold (solved if they die as well), and risking obsolescence; successful captures can then increase economy, unit support, and long-term growth; and the needless wait was very problematic and inefficient too. See [KI#156](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#156---fixed-base-advciv-issue-ready-city-attack-stacks-could-park-for-future-upgrades-even-when-no-unit-could-upgrade-now).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5954+) **AI unit upgrades - stop routine upgrades from draining the treasury:** Fixes an AI spending problem where normal unit **upgrades could overshoot the intended upgrade budget**. Previously, the AI only checked whether it was still below budget before starting the next upgrade, but did not check whether that next upgrade itself fit the remaining budget. For example, in BBAI diagnostics, an AI with 280 gold reserved for upgrades could spend 237 gold, still count as under budget, then start another 237 gold upgrade and end up spending 474 gold total, sometimes leaving only a few gold in the treasury. Routine **upgrades now check the actual remaining upgrade budget before upgrading**. **Emergency**-style upgrades, such as important city defenders or special impassable-unit upgrades, **remain more flexible**, but normal upgrades are much less likely to waste all available gold. AIs in **financial trouble also avoid** routine upgrades **unless they are focused on war**. See [KI#160](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#160---fixed-likely-inherited-ai-upgrade-budget-issue-normal-upgrades-could-overshoot-the-remaining-budget-and-leave-the-ai-almost-broke).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5952+) **AI attack stacks now opportunistically capture nearby barbarian cities:** before this fix, ready city-attack-capable (i.e. not just `UNITAI_ATTACK_CITY`) stacks could sit idle because the AI had **no normal war target yet**, even with a **nearby pathable barbarian city**. Now, if the AI has **no normal target** and is **not already busy with an active war**, the stack can **attack that barbarian city instead**. This does not override normal wars or normal war targets; it fills the no-target idle gap. This change is usually a **strong** move: barbarian cities are often weak, cost little diplomatically, and capturing them can give land, population, resources, unit support, and future growth without spending a settler or interrupting city production nor growth. In the tested save before the fix, nearby barbarian cities survived at game end at ~T400 despite reachable AI armies to Napoleon and Shaka, and barbarian being very weak in comparison, yet bonuses-rich, and Shaka was among weakest players; after the fix, Zulu captured Hurrian at T127, Alemanni at T132, Visigoth at T148, and Gaul at T153, turning those nearby barb cities into an early expansion boost and making Shaka the strongest player for almost all middle game and end game, almost allowing him to win. This change should make AI significantly stronger. See [KI#158](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#158---fixed-base-advciv-issue-ready-no-target-attack-stacks-could-ignore-pathable-barbarian-cities-while-only-preparing-a-future-war).
 
